# F104 CAM++ checkpoint-weight licence determination

Date: 2026-08-25  
Stream: F104 (`kilix-transcribe` diarization route)  
Scope: exact ModelScope CAM++ checkpoint weights, not 3D-Speaker code, example audio, mirrors, or other model families  
Method: read-only primary-source inspection; no model weight was downloaded or copied

## 1. Determination

**The two exact CAM++ checkpoint artifacts below do carry an Apache-2.0
grant.** The grant does not rest on the 3D-Speaker code licence. It rests on the
explicit `license: Apache License 2.0` declaration committed in each official
IIC ModelScope *model repository*, combined with ModelScope's primary
documentation defining a model as the architecture plus its corresponding
parameters and saying the selected licence determines the open-source protocol
that the model follows.

That is sufficient under S3 to treat the declaration as applying to the
checkpoint parameters rather than merely to surrounding code. The absence of a
standalone `LICENSE` file is still a distribution defect to cure: a selected
Plebian/Kilix artifact must carry the complete Apache-2.0 text and the evidence
that binds that text to the exact checkpoint.

This determination is deliberately digest-specific. It does **not** preserve
the earlier wildcard clearance for `iic/speech_campplus_*` or
`iic/speech_eres2net*`; unlisted checkpoints remain unknown until read in this
same form.

## 2. Why this is a weight grant rather than a code-licence inference

Three primary sources form the grant chain:

1. The official IIC ModelScope repositories contain the checkpoint (or its Git
   LFS pointer), configuration and model card in one versioned model tree. At the
   exact revisions below, the model card explicitly contains
   `license: Apache License 2.0`. Neither determination is inferred from the
   separate `modelscope/3D-Speaker` GitHub `LICENSE`.
2. ModelScope's official [Model Hub introduction](https://www.modelscope.cn/docs/models/intro)
   defines “Model” as a model instance including its architecture and
   corresponding parameters. The same document says the licence type determines
   which open-source protocol the model follows. Thus the repository-level
   licence field is stated by the hosting authority to cover the model
   parameters, not just SDK or example code.
3. The referenced standard is the official
   [Apache License 2.0 text](https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0.txt),
   11,358 bytes, SHA-256
   `cfc7749b96f63bd31c3c42b5c471bf756814053e847c10f3eb003417bc523d30`.
   Its copyright and patent grants permit reproduction, derivative works and
   redistribution subject to its conditions and contain no field-of-use or
   voice-cloning restriction.

The ModelScope API independently reports `License: Apache License 2.0` for the
[English checkpoint](https://www.modelscope.cn/api/v1/models/iic/speech_campplus_sv_en_voxceleb_16k)
and [Chinese checkpoint](https://www.modelscope.cn/api/v1/models/iic/speech_campplus_sv_zh-cn_16k-common).
Both API records identify the public IIC organization and describe the entries
as user-uploaded models. `LicenseLink` and `LicenseName` are empty, so the API is
corroboration of the committed model-card declaration, not a substitute licence
instrument.

ModelScope documentation notes that an unspecified SDK creation value can
default to Apache-2.0. That does not make these rows default-derived: the exact
README bytes in both repositories explicitly contain the licence declaration,
and it persists at the checkpoint revisions inspected.

